INT. TAXIDERMY WORKSHOP - NIGHT

Glass eyes glitter from every shelf. A stuffed owl, a lynx mid-leap, a badger with a crooked grin.

BEATRIX CALLOWAY, 50s, magnifier on her forehead, kneels by an open drawer while NICO ABARA, 20s, apprentice, holds the lantern and FENWICK, 70s, owner, stands rigid at the door, fingers twitching, and all three have noticed that the fox on the counter is missing its left eye.

BEATRIX
Fenwick, you said nobody came in.

FENWICK
Nobody did, Beatrix.

NICO
The fox, Mr. Fenwick. Look at the fox.

Beatrix lifts the animal, turns it, peers into the empty socket, pulls a folded note from inside, and Nico leans in, lantern shaking, while Fenwick edges toward the back door, one hand in his coat.

BEATRIX
Stop there, Fenwick.

FENWICK
It's a misunderstanding.

NICO
It says "the eye is in the owl."

They all turn. The owl stares back.
